;; FH-ROUTELINK-004 — the server shell: a real href, and no handler.
;;
;; Server-rendered HTML has to carry working links: the crawler, the
;; no-JavaScript reader and the first paint all navigate by `href` alone.
;; So the JVM render emits the same real anchor with the same href — in
;; PATH form, because a server has no address bar and a hash never reaches
;; it, and the hydrated client re-encodes through the frame's URL strategy.
;;
;; And it emits NO click handler. A server tree that carried one would be
;; carrying a host closure into a serialized document — there is no click
;; to intercept on the server, and nothing may pretend otherwise.
{:fh/id "FH-ROUTELINK-004"
 :fh/law
 "The JVM/SSR render emits the same real anchor with the path-form href and NO click handler — a server document has no click to intercept."

 :routes
 {:fh.route/cart    "/cart"
  :fh.route/article "/articles/:slug"}

 :cases
 [{:why   "a plain route"
   :props {:to :fh.route/cart}
   :href  "/cart"}
  {:why   "params and query survive into the server shell"
   :props {:to :fh.route/article :params {:slug "the-intro"} :query {:tab "notes"}}
   :href  "/articles/the-intro?tab=notes"}]

 ;; Keys that must NOT appear on the server anchor's attribute map.
 :absent-attrs [:on-click]

 ;; Passthrough attributes still render server-side: the shell is a real
 ;; anchor, not a placeholder the client replaces.
 :passthrough
 {:props {:to :fh.route/cart :class "nav" :aria-label "Cart"}
  :attrs {:class "nav" :aria-label "Cart"}}}
